I wonder if making this decision at bind time might be too coarse? At bind time we only know which columns are assigned, not whether the referenced key actually changes, so something like

```sql
UPDATE test SET id = id WHERE id = 1;
```

still fails with this fix. Perhaps we can make this decision in PhysicalUpdate::Sink instead? On the delete + insert path it already has both the old and the new version of each row before calling table.Delete.

Similarly, when a table is referenced through two different keys:

```sql
CREATE TABLE p  (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, code VARCHAR UNIQUE);
CREATE TABLE c1 (pid INTEGER REFERENCES p(id));
CREATE TABLE c2 (code VARCHAR REFERENCES p(code));
INSERT INTO p VALUES (1, 'A');
INSERT INTO c1 VALUES (1);

UPDATE p SET code = 'B' WHERE id = 1;
```

This should succeed: id doesn't change, and nothing references code = 'A'. But because code is assigned, the flag is off for the whole statement, so c1's check on id = 1 still runs and fails.

