Could you resolve `first` through the catalog here, in line with #25472 and #25602? The direct factory call bypasses catalog lookup, leaving the introduced aggregate without its catalog/schema identity. The helper could become:

```suggestion
static unique_ptr<BoundAggregateExpression> BindFirst(ClientContext &context, unique_ptr<Expression> child) {
	auto first_function =
	    GetBuiltinAggregateFunction(context, FirstFun::Name, {child->GetReturnType()});
	vector<unique_ptr<Expression>> children;
	children.push_back(std::move(child));
	return first_function->Bind(context, std::move(children));
}
```

This also needs `distributive_functions.hpp` for `FirstFun` and the `builtin_function_lookup.hpp` header. The lookup helper is already on `v2.0-cyanoptera`; its header moves from `duckdb/optimizer/` to `duckdb/function/` in #25602, so the include depends on the base used.

Thanks - clanker suggested this as further potential improvements, maybe you can give it a shot:

```cpp
#include <cmath>

// Clang on AArch64 lowers these to fminnm/fmax, which it can vectorize as reductions
#if defined(__clang__) && defined(__aarch64__) && __has_builtin(__builtin_elementwise_maximum)
#define DUCKDB_NATIVE_FLOAT_MINMAX
#endif

//! NaN is ignored unless both inputs are NaN (IEEE 754-2008 minNum)
template <class T>
inline T MinFloatingPoint(T left, T right) {
#ifdef DUCKDB_NATIVE_FLOAT_MINMAX
      return std::fmin(left, right);
#else
      if (std::isnan(left)) {
              return right;
      }
      if (std::isnan(right)) {
              return left;
      }
      return right > left ? left : right;
#endif
}

//! NaN propagates, since NaN sorts greater than everything (IEEE 754-2019 maximum)
template <class T>
inline T MaxFloatingPoint(T left, T right) {
#ifdef DUCKDB_NATIVE_FLOAT_MINMAX
      return __builtin_elementwise_maximum(left, right);
#else
      if (std::isnan(left)) {
              return left;
      }
      if (std::isnan(right)) {
              return right;
      }
      return right < left ? left : right;
#endif
}
```

