Allocating the filter from estimated build cardinality can make a previously successful query fail before any build rows arrive:

```sql
SET threads=1;
CREATE TABLE p AS SELECT range % 2 AS k FROM range(20000000);
CHECKPOINT;
SET memory_limit='25MB';

SELECT count(*) FROM p
WHERE k IN (
    SELECT range FROM range(100000000)
    WHERE range % 50000000=0
);
```

The build is estimated at 20 million rows but produces only two. The parent returns `10000000`. This revision fails with `Out of Memory Error: failed to allocate data of size 32.0 MiB`. Disabling `join_filter_pushdown` also makes the query succeed on this revision.

Folding in `Seal()` cannot help because the initial allocation already failed. Could we bound this allocation by the available memory budget or skip the optional bloom filter when it cannot fit, and add an overestimated-build regression alongside the underestimated-build case?

