Well, the bitmap already lives in `SelectionVector` — `SelectionData` contains bitmap_data/row_span/is_bitmap, and data(), operator[] and Sort() already flatten on access via Flatten(). The only accessor that doesn't is `get_index(),` which asserts instead.

So do you want get_index() to flatten automatically? That's a lot of call sites, in the hottest loops (join_hashtable, update_segment, comparison_operators, tuple_data_scatter_gather, plus the executors).

Assuming the answer is no, a stray bitmap poses a silent regression (not a compile error). This is why I applied inheritance with SelectionResult such that a filter-oriented operator can opt in safely.

Note that some operators use selvecs as mappings, not filters: DictionaryVector::SelVector, the build-row-id vector in EmitDictVectors (join_hashtable.cpp:2838), sort permutations — duplicates, arbitrary order, domain unrelated to count. These cannot be bitmaps.

Maybe another approach is to explicitly extract the selvec in some struct, that one can call get_index() or simply [] on.  I stayed away from that because I already have so many LoC changed and did not want more.
```
enum class SelectionVectorType : uint8_t { INCREMENTAL, INDEX_BASED, BITMAP_BASED };

//! Index-based view of a selection — the form hot loops consume. Borrows; owns nothing.
struct FlatSelection {
      inline idx_t operator[](idx_t i) const { 
           D_ASSERT(!sel || i < capacity); 
           return sel ? sel[i] : i; 
      }
      const sel_t *data() const { return sel; }
      sel_t *data() { return sel; }
      bool IsIncremental() const { return sel == nullptr; }
private:
      friend class SelectionVector;
      sel_t *sel = nullptr;
      idx_t capacity = 0;
};

class SelectionVector {
public:
      FlatSelection Flatten();  //! Materialize BITMAP_BASED -> INDEX_BASED. 
      bool IsSet() const { return sel_type != SelectionVectorType::INCREMENTAL; }
private:
      SelectionVectorType sel_type = SelectionVectorType::INCREMENTAL;
      FlatSelection flat;                      // primary state; may point outside selection_data
      buffer_ptr<SelectionData> selection_data; // optional owner of flat.sel and/or the bitmap
};
```

