@Mytherin conflicts are resolved — the branch is rebased onto current `main` (`8806a9f863`) and now merges cleanly.

The conflict came from `BloomFilterExcludes()`, which landed on `main` with the signed-zero fix in exactly the spot this PR was editing. Rather than replay the old commits, I rebased and folded the NaN check into that helper, next to the ±0.0 case — it's the same underlying issue (floating-point equality semantics not matching bit-pattern hashing), so it belongs in one place:

```cpp
case PhysicalType::DOUBLE: {
	auto double_value = constant.GetValue<double>();
	if (Value::IsNan(double_value)) {
		return false;
	}
	if (double_value == 0.0) {
		return !bloom_filter.FilterCheck(ValueXH64FixedWidth(0.0)) &&
		       !bloom_filter.FilterCheck(ValueXH64FixedWidth(-0.0));
	}
	break;
}
```

This keeps the behaviour you asked for in review: pruning is skipped entirely for NaN constants rather than forcing the hash to zero, so files written by non-DuckDB writers using a different NaN representation are handled correctly. `ValueXXH64()` is untouched and remains hashing-only.

The test moved to sit alongside the signed-zero block and mirrors its structure — NaN payloads in row group 0, ±1.0 in row group 1 — covering both `= 'NaN'` and `IS NOT DISTINCT FROM 'NaN'`.

